#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# timing

#% text
# `timing(e)` evaluates `e` and gives `[seconds, value]`: the wall-clock time the evaluation took and its value.

#% text
# `timing(e, breakdown: true)` gives `[seconds, value, parts]`, with `parts` an association of where the time went.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The time is in seconds, as a machine real; it depends on the machine and its load, so pages and tests should not rely on its value.

#% item
# The expression is held and evaluated once, inside the timing.

#% item
# Printed output of the evaluation appears as usual.

#% item
# The breakdown's parts are `"compiled"` (seconds in compiled code), `"library"` (in functions built into the system), `"interpreter"` (the rest: evaluating definitions and expressions), `"gc"` (collections; none run inside an evaluation, they run between statements, so it is 0) and `"allocated_mb"` (memory taken by new terms, in megabytes). In a parallel evaluation the times are added over the threads. `profile` gives the same measurements per function.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# The value is the second element:

#% cell
r = timing(sum(range(10^5)))
r[2]
#% output
#= 5000050000

#% cell
is_real(r[1]) and r[1] >= 0
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Any expression, including a block:

#% cell
timing({ let a = 2^1000; integer_length(a) })[2]
#% output
#= 302

#% subsection
# Options

#% text
# Where the time of a numerical integral goes:

#% cell
keys(timing(n_integrate(exp(-x^2), [x, 0, 3]), breakdown: true)[3])
#% output
#= ["compiled", "library", "interpreter", "gc", "allocated_mb"]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# Stage times of a script, as the reheating and MCMC scripts report them: time each stage and keep its value.

#% cell
stage = timing(n_integrate(exp(-x^2), [x, -inf, inf]))
q = stage[2]
q
#% output
#= 1.7724538509055163

#% text
# A comparison of two ways to compute the same value: both values agree.

#% cell
t1 = timing(total(range(10^4)))
t2 = timing(sum(k, [k, 1, 10^4]))
t1[2] == t2[2]
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The value is what the expression gives without `timing`:

#% cell
timing(factor(x^4 - 1))[2] === factor(x^4 - 1)
#% output
#= true

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `say`, `print`, `profile`, `n_integrate`, `n_dsolve`
